ns are under investigation for organized crime links, declaring that \"nobody who is not honest can hide beneath the halo of the Transformation.\" The third track was programmatic: she inaugurated what her government described as the largest hospital in Latin America — the 669-bed Hospital O'Horán in Mérida, with 16 operating theaters and 48 specialties — and announced a 181 billion peso hospital investment plan for her presidency. \"When there is no corruption, the money is enough.\"\n\nThe unconfirmed report that Morena senator Enrique Inzunza Cazarez is also in US custody — denied by Mr Inzunza and not confirmed by US authorities — could extend the crisis into the legislature. Interim governor Yeraldine Bonilla added a further complication, claiming that SEDENA, not Rocha Moya, had appointed the now-arrested security chief Gerardo Mérida Sánchez to his post. The military has neither confirmed nor denied this. SEDENA's silence more than 48 hours after a specific, attributable claim is itself a signal worth watching.\n\nPEMEX is deteriorating on a separate but parallel track. Ms Sheinbaum replaced chief executive Víctor Rodríguez Padilla — an energy academic — with chief financial officer Juan Carlos Carpio Fragoso, an economist who worked with her during her time running Mexico City. The appointment signals that debt management has displaced production recovery as the company's priority: output fell 6% under Mr Rodríguez to 1.65 million barrels a day, and debt stands at $79 billion. S&P this week revised its outlook on Pemex, CFE, and their subsidiaries to negative — the day after doing the same to the Mexican sovereign — and called PEMEX's standalone credit profile \"unsustainable.\" Public accounts documents revealed that 67% of PEMEX's supplier debt — more than 375 billion pesos, of which over 250 billion has been pushed to 2033 — was quietly restructured during Mr Rodríguez's tenure, converting short-term payables into eight-year obligations. Banamex warned that Moody's may follow S&P on the sovereign.\n\nAgainst all this, Economy Secretary Marcelo Ebrard confirmed that Mexico will sign the Global Modernized Agreement with the European Union on May 22 — the most concrete step toward trade diversification of the Sheinbaum presidency. The Senate approved a formal session to receive EU Council President António Costa. The agreement liberalizes 86% of agricultural trade and extends to manufacturing, energy, and technology. It does not offset the structural weight of US dependency, but it will be real and signed.\n\nThirty-two days from the first World Cup group-stage match in Guadalajara, there is still no public security framework for the games in Jalisco — CJNG's home state, the theater most disrupted by the post-El Mencho fragmentation. The gap between the most internationally visible security test of this presidency and any visible preparation for it grows harder to explain.",
